# ── Rights-floor firewall pins ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Run by `just verify-pins` (nibli-pin, native — no wasm, no fuel).
#
# WHAT THIS GUARDS, and why it lives in the ENGINE repo rather than next to the
# prose it serves: a constitutional knowledge base spells an unconditional right
# as `entitled(every person, event { P() }).` and then depends on the engine
# REFUSING any rule that would punish the absence of P. That refusal is EMERGENT.
# It falls out of an asymmetry in nibli-reason:
#
#   * `rules::flatten_consequent` descends BOTH branches of the head's `And`, so
#     an abstraction body's `P` becomes a rule-head conclusion and therefore a
#     dependency edge `P -> person`;
#   * `rules::collect_ground_facts` DOES honour `__abs_` opacity and skips that
#     body, so the floor never derives that anyone actually does P.
#
# Make those two symmetric in either direction and something breaks: drop the
# opacity guard and an entitlement starts fabricating the actuality it
# guarantees; honour opacity in the head walk too and every firewall pin below
# silently starts LOADING. No test in a prose repo could ever fire on that
# refactor, which is the whole reason these pins are hosted here.
#
# The pins are deliberately expressed as REFUSALS with an error CLASS plus a
# message needle. `:refuse reasoning /…/` cannot be satisfied by a misspelled
# predicate, because a typo is a `Syntax` error and the class is matched on the
# NibliError variant, not on the rendered string.

# ── the constitution under test ──
# Article 1's unconditional floor. Kept in this file rather than loaded from
# utopia.nibli so a pin can never be quietly weakened by an edit elsewhere.
entitled(every person, event { eats() }).
entitled(every person, event { dwell() }).
entitled(every person, event { learn() }).

# Prisoners remain persons. This closure link is what lets a punishing rule
# reach back into the floor's protected set and close the negative cycle;
# without it every firewall pin below would load and prove nothing.
all $anyone: prisoner($anyone) -> person($anyone).

# ── THE FIREWALL: no rule may punish the absence of a floor right ──
:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'eats'/
all $x: person($x) & ~eats($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'dwell'/
all $x: person($x) & ~dwell($x) -> prisoner($x).

:refuse reasoning /'prisoner' -> 'learn'/
all $x: person($x) & ~learn($x) -> prisoner($x).

# The refusal must be a STRATIFICATION refusal specifically, not merely "some
# reasoning error" — pin the diagnostic itself so a future error-message change
# cannot turn this suite into a tautology.
:refuse reasoning /Unstratifiable negation/
all $x: person($x) & ~eats($x) -> prisoner($x).

# ── COMPLEMENT CONTROLS: the protected set is EXACTLY the floor ──
# These are the other half of the guarantee. Refusals alone are satisfiable by an
# engine that refuses everything; an over-broad protected set is just as much a
# bug as an absent one, because it silently converts an ordinary predicate into
# an unconditional right. Each of these MUST still load.
:accept
all $x: person($x) & ~home($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept
all $x: person($x) & ~choose($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept
all $x: person($x) & ~permits($x) -> prisoner($x).

:accept
all $x: person($x) & ~false($x) -> prisoner($x).

# NOT in the control set, deliberately: `~travel` is refused too, but for a
# reason that has nothing to do with the floor — utopia.nibli's Article 2 derives
# travel behind `~prisoner`, so `~travel -> prisoner` closes its own negative
# cycle. Listing it as a control would pass for the wrong reason and would keep
# passing after the firewall broke. Left here as prose, not as a pin.

# ── OPACITY: an entitlement must not fabricate the actuality ──
person(Adam).

? entitled(Adam, event { eats() }).
# => TRUE

? eats(Adam).
# => FALSE

? eats(some person).
# => FALSE

# A right nobody was granted stays absent — the floor is not a blanket TRUE.
? entitled(Adam, event { choose() }).
# => FALSE

# Anti-hollowing floor: raise or lower this consciously in the same diff that
# adds or removes a pin.
:expect-pins 12
